the departure from Israel marks the end  of a long battle by Salah hamuri to stay  in the country he has always called home  he's been imprisoned several times once  for his alleged involvement in the  assassination plot of a former Chief  Rabbi for which he got seven years and  was branded a terrorist by Israel as a  member of the popular front for the  liberation of Palestine  his Jerusalem residency was revoked and  as a dual National with French  citizenship that opened the way for his  deportation to France  the effect made it easier for Israel to  get rid of him  we'll send him to France  un experts say the deportation of  protected persons from an occupied  territory could constitute a war crime  and his supporters believe his case  could Now set a precedent for Israel to  deport other activists the case has also  highlighted the controversial practice  of administrative detention which omori  has been held under since March it  allows Israeli authorities to keep  suspects locked up without charge and  without explaining the evidence against  them hamuri's parents who both live in  occupied East Jerusalem say they have  never been told why he's been imprisoned  this time  they just say vague things that he is a  danger for the security of the country  but they don't give us concrete things  rights groups say more than 800  Palestinians are being held in the same  way in Israeli prisons Rob McBride Al  Jazeera West Jerusalem
 

